index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..f25f1a0386aeb8b2f5ea0b6e29d0b7b47e714733
--- /dev/null
+++ b/packages/coding-agent/docs/execution-ownership.md
@@ -0,0 +1,132 @@
+# Execution ownership — source preparation
+
+The `0010-execution-ownership.patch` preparation is **incomplete and unaccepted**. It is authored after patches
+1–6 only, not a final 1–10 combination. No OS bridge artifact, dispatcher backend, shared-service policy, cleanup
+capability or ordinary global installation is admitted. `omp --execution-dispatcher` currently rejects with
+`ownership_backend_unavailable` before the ordinary runtime import. That refusal is intentional, not a working server.
+
+## Chosen installation contract
+
+The supported compatible Native installation will use an independently running public dispatcher for **all**
+shared-broker creation, including ordinary TUI and nested `env -i ... omp`. Ordinary clients will never start a
+dispatcher or broker locally. Host may bootstrap the dispatcher before starting any private helper; operators may
+start it independently. Missing, legacy, mismatched and failed peers fail closed. This startup dependency changes
+ordinary TUI deployment and must be accepted as part of the selected installation, never inferred from a profile flag.
+
+The rendezvous will derive from an immutable selected installation identity and actual UID, independent of HOME,
+XDG, cwd, profile and inherited environment. Its canonical private directory/socket and maintenance control are
+outside replaceable release files. Generation/closure/schema validation precedes broker publication. The backend
+that establishes that rendezvous and protection is **not implemented** in this preparation.
+
+## Authored public modules
+
+`launch/ownership/protocol` defines `omp-execution-ownership/1`, strict request/response decoders and typed errors.
+`wire.schema.json` contains the matching public draft-07 request/response schemas. Use
+`#/definitions/request`, `#/definitions/response`, `#/definitions/helperRequest` and `#/definitions/helperResponse`;
+framing byte limits and correlation are additional requirements.
+`framing` accepts one LF-terminated UTF-8 frame at a time, rejects malformed UTF-8/oversize/incomplete EOF and
+forbids pipelining. `client` validates request correlation, release/schema, operation and immutable process registration.
+Private registration refuses broker acquisition and publication before another wire request. It has no local spawn API.
+
+`dispatcher` is the decision module behind the authenticated OS/storage seam. It classifies each actual peer again
+on every request, including reconnect/publication. A retained process identity must continue to classify identically.
+Its public wire operations are fixed:
+
+- `classify`: no PID, environment or process-name authority from a caller; return immutable private/external registration.
+- `broker`: canonical project directory or fixed `browser-relay`/`text-predict` global scope; no executable/argv choice.
+- `publish`: exact dispatcher-owned broker peer, scope and generation, validated before any broker socket publication.
+- `prepare_helper` / `helper_status`: bind a generation to the actual external Host before spawning; only that Host
+  may observe its pending/registered state. Neither prepare nor a pending status grants worker START.
+
+Helper registration uses the separate `omp-execution-helper/1` version at the same fixed endpoint, at most 512 bytes
+including LF. The helper actively connects with a retained socket before HELLO; inherited fd3 socketpair credentials
+cannot substitute for this actual helper peer. Requests carry installation, generation, operation and an independent
+random 64-hex request ID, distinct from generation and the previous operation. Canonical success is exactly
+`{"version":"omp-execution-helper/1","id":"<requestId>","ok":true,"operation":"register_helper|complete_helper","generation":"<generation>"}`
+plus LF. The C helper compares exact bytes. Late/duplicate REGISTER success cannot match COMPLETE. Retain this socket
+through completion; loss fences shared registration and preserves the helper's current private policy/lease/fence.
+Only explicit force, mandatory shutdown or Host fd3 loss triggers the corresponding private cleanup escalation.
+
+Only the trusted actual-helper seam may register/complete a private helper. `registerHelper` returns only after
+the system adapter verifies the actual helper and supervisor, retains stable identities and durably fsyncs the
+incomplete generation. Only then may Host release the helper's worker-start barrier. PID/starttime fields in control
+receipts are audit/correlation hints. They are not authority; prefer the helper's own authenticated dispatcher connection
+to obtain SO_PEERPIDFD. The accepted order is Host prepare before spawn, helper registration durable ACK before HELLO,
+Host status verification before START. COMPLETE is sent only after root reap + authoritative ECHILD; its durable ACK
+precedes the helper clearing its own fence/exiting. `helperUncertain` fences new shared registrations globally before awaited storage work.
+There is no client recovery/reset operation. Completion requires the actual trusted helper's extinction proof.
+
+`OwnershipSystem` is an OS/durable-storage seam, not an existing production implementation. Its test adapter models
+kernel and disk facts; it proves decision behavior only. It does not prove ancestry, environment clearing, maintenance
+protection, actual service preservation or process extinction. A backend must check the synchronous publication permit
+after every awaited prepare and at each spawn/publication barrier; aborted/unknown backend outcomes retain fences.
+
+## Linux syscall bridge source
+
+`linux-bridge.c` and `linux-bridge.ts` prepare ABI 1. The C library owns listener/client sockets, peer pidfds and
+temporary ancestor pidfds; FFI uses generational table handles and opaque object identities, not private Bun socket
+fields. It requires actual SO_PEERPIDFD plus SO_PEERCRED, retains peer identity, walks `/proc` with retained parent
+pidfds, then rechecks every identity and parent edge. Missing, changed, namespace-mismatched or over-limit chains
+are unresolved. Classification grants no signal/wait authority. No fallback to SO_PEERCRED numeric PID alone exists.
+
+The bridge is loaded only explicitly by a future execution-ownership backend. Its selection requires an independent
+library SHA256, canonical direct file, ABI check and independently held/rechecked maintenance protection. The final
+closure must include its source, compiler executable identity, exact arguments/target, resulting bytes and platform
+libraries. Strict compilation and one bounded socket/pidfd fixture are recorded separately. Actual SO_PEERPIDFD,
+self-peer identity, byte transfer and descriptor release passed; positive helper parentage, lifecycle and kernel-fault
+coverage remain unproven. This primitive result does not admit the dispatcher/backend. A kernel version string cannot
+establish support. Catalog/preflight must not import the bridge.
+
+## Fixed budgets
+
+| Resource | Limit / outcome |
+| --- | --- |
+| Physical UTF-8 frame | 16,384 bytes excluding LF; reject and close |
+| Connection receive/output queue | 32,768 bytes each; reject/close, no unbounded retries |
+| Connections / pending requests | 64 total / 1 per connection; no pipelining |
+| Global concurrent dispatcher operation / queue | 1 / 0; immediate typed refusal |
+| Retained process registrations / private helpers | 512 / 64; refuse new admission on exhaustion |
+| Helper generations per dispatcher / pending + registered helpers | 512 / 64; no generation reuse or silent eviction |
+| Ancestor depth | 256 retained identities per traversal; overflow unresolved |
+| C table slots / aggregate owned descriptors | 1,024 / 2,048; overflow fails closed, release partial acquisitions |
+| Broker scopes / independently protected external lifetimes | 64 / 256; refuse overflow |
+| Connect / request / idle deadline | 1,000 ms / 10,000 ms / 30,000 ms |
+| Bridge artifact | At most 1 MiB, canonical regular single-link file, independently selected SHA256 |
+
+The concrete transport/backend must enforce connection/output/deadline and lifetime limits; pure decoders do not
+implement an OS server. Do not evict an unresolved helper/lifetime or close its protector to make a resource test pass.
+Timeout is an unanswered operation, never proof that no broker/service was created. Persist uncertainty and block reuse.
+
+## Required consumer policy and supervision (not yet integrated)
+
+Hosted Native and nested compatible Native below the registered private helper will receive immutable private-only
+policy before Session/UI/import/factory effects. Generic bash, private background jobs and native parent-owned subagents
+remain supported. Named shared service administration, browser/relay, IDA and text-prediction old-daemon control fail
+with `shared_service_unsupported` before token/mkdir/request/completion effects. Private LSP bypasses external lspmux;
+image blobs use the actual local fallback. Every mandatory attachment format/budget/consumption path still needs evidence.
+
+All broker factory/connect/reconnect paths must lose automatic spawn, and the legacy broker public entry must reject
+without the dispatcher-owned generation handshake before publication. The current baseline client still auto-spawns;
+those edits and policy enforcement are pending. Do not infer them from the new client module having no spawn method.
+
+The dedicated C private subreaper is the sole wait owner of Native and adopted descendants; Bun waits only for it.
+Native stdio stays direct. A separate authenticated bounded control channel reports actual Native PID/generation,
+with a pre-exec/start barrier and lifetime identity. Native leases/runtime mapping checks use that actual Native PID.
+Private control EOF initiates fail-safe cleanup; unresolved children retain helper/lease/fence. Root reap, empty proc
+children and process groups do not prove completion: root reaped + closed admission + authoritative P_ALL/ECHILD do.
+
+External brokers and every surviving service need independent protectors outside private helper domains. An explicit
+external policy must preserve services on dispatcher/broker/control EOF while keeping lease and durable incomplete
+fence until actual extinction. Private EOF cleanup cannot be reused for that purpose. Broker-only leases are insufficient.
+
+## Validation gate
+
+Pure public module/schema tests are source evidence only. Next required results are bounded harmless OS bridge and
+helper fixtures, including peerpidfd failure, stale/foreign identities, descriptor exhaustion/leaks, changing ancestry,
+setsid/double-fork/nested subreaper, helper EOF/crash and unresolved fences. Then use actual synthetic Native bash,
+background/subagents, nested env-i creation/reconnect denial with zero publication, independent external TUI/service
+preservation, missing/legacy dispatcher negatives, private LSP and all mandatory attachment paths. Rebuild final same-source
+schema/client/server and repeat the matched actual-global combination. Linux first; actual WSL2 remains owner-deferred.
+
+Arbitrary downloaded old OMP, malicious same-UID/ptrace/namespace/reparent manipulation and externally delegated
+irreversible effects are outside this supported lifecycle guarantee. Generic bash is not an OS sandbox or external-effects rollback.
